# Calculators and the spreadsheets, one stanza per program.

shot    loan
size    46 80
cap     An amortisation calculator.  It asks for the principal, the term
cap     in years, the yearly rate and the month payments start, and
cap     prints the monthly payment, the total interest, and a schedule
cap     of every month's principal, interest and balance, subtotalled
cap     at each year end.  Payments from August give a five-month first
cap     year.  The schedule runs the full term, so page it or cut it.
try     loan | head -n 40
os9     loan
run     loan | head -n 40
wait    3
send    120000\r
wait    2
send    30\r
wait    2
send    6.5\r
wait    2
send    8\r
wait    8

shot    cvtbase
cap     Converts numbers from one base to another.  The two arguments
cap     name the bases, in and out: d decimal, h or x hex, o octal, b
cap     binary, or the digit characters themselves for any other base.
cap     Each number typed is answered on the next line; Escape ends it.
try     cvtbase d h
run     cvtbase d h
wait    3
send    255\r
wait    2
send    4095\r
wait    2
send    \033
wait    2
run     cvtbase h b
wait    3
send    ff\r
wait    2
send    \033
wait    2

shot    rechne
cap     A German command-line calculator that gives every answer three
cap     ways: decimal, hex and binary.  The expression is one argument
cap     with no spaces; the operators are + - x / and m for modulo, a, o
cap     and p for and, or and xor, and $ prefixes a hex number.  -b lists
cap     which bits of the result are set.
try     rechne 4095+1
run     rechne 4095+1
wait    2
run     rechne 12x12
wait    2
run     rechne \$ff+1
wait    2
run     rechne 100m7
wait    2
run     rechne -b 12345
wait    3

shot    rpn
size    50 80
cap     12 and 34 added, the sum times 100, then divided by 7: after each line
cap     rpn shows the top of the stack and how deep it is.  `?' lists the
cap     words it knows; q leaves.
try     rpn
run     rpn
wait    2
send    12\r
wait    1
send    34\r
wait    1
send    add\r
wait    1
send    100\r
wait    1
send    mul\r
wait    1
send    7\r
wait    1
send    div\r
wait    1
send    ?\r
wait    2
snap
send    q\r
wait    2

shot    checkfile
size    30 80
cap     Two records added through its form -- an electric bill entered as a
cap     cheque, a pay cheque as a deposit -- and R listing them with the
cap     balance.  Records go in testfile.dat in the current directory.
try     checkfile
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MTHB > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MTHB
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MTHB
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     checkfile
wait    5
send    A
wait    3
send    101\r
wait    2
send    26/09/01\r
wait    2
send    CHK\r
wait    2
send    Electric bill\r
wait    2
send    UTIL\r
wait    2
send    -84.50\r
wait    3
send    C
wait    3
send    Y
wait    3
send    102\r
wait    2
send    26/09/03\r
wait    2
send    DEP\r
wait    2
send    Pay cheque\r
wait    2
send    INC\r
wait    2
send    1250.00\r
wait    3
send    C
wait    3
send    N
wait    3
send    R
wait    5
snap
send    \r
wait    3
quit    Q

shot    oleo
size    24 80
cap     GNU Oleo 1.6, a spreadsheet, past its opening notice: 6*7 typed into
cap     the first cell and its value shown there.  Its help character is ^H.
try     oleo
run     clear
wait    1
run     oleo
wait    6
send    \s
wait    4
send    =6*7\r
wait    4
snap
quit    \030\003yes\r
wait    2

shot    scqref
cap     Page it with less: space for the next page, q to leave.
try     scqref | less
os9     scqref ! less
run     clear
wait    1
run     scqref | less
wait    10
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    factor
size    24 80
cap     factor prints the prime factors of each number it is given.
try     factor 1234567890 1000001 97
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     factor 1234567890 1000001 97
wait    5

shot    primes
size    30 80
cap     The twenty-five primes up to 100, one to a line.
try     primes 1 100
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     primes 1 100
wait    5

shot    number
size    24 80
cap     1234567 in English words, a part to a line; the `...' is number's
cap     own mark for the end of a number.
try     number 1234567
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     number 1234567
wait    5

shot    dc
size    24 80
cap     The integer desk calculator after 6*7: the display top right, five
cap     memory slots down the left, and the keypad laid out like the Atari
cap     ST's.  DCGRAPHIC names six plain characters to draw the boxes with;
cap     without it the frame is drawn in characters your terminal may not
cap     have.  Q or ESC leaves it.
try     DCGRAPHIC='++++|-' dc
run     clear
wait    1
run     DCGRAPHIC='++++|-' dc
wait    5
send    6*7\r
wait    3
snap
send    Q
wait    3

shot    bc
size    14 80
cap     An arbitrary-precision calculator: each sum above its answer.  Two to
cap     the two-hundredth comes out in full, a seventh to forty decimal
cap     places, and pi as four times the arctangent of one -- `scale' says
cap     how many places to keep, and -l loads the maths library that a(1)
cap     comes from.  Run on its own, bc answers line by line until `quit'.
try     echo "2^200" | bc
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "2^200" | bc
wait    4
run     echo "scale=40; 1/7" | bc
wait    4
run     echo "scale=20; a(1)*4" | bc -l
wait    5
snap

shot    chemtab
size    24 80
cap     Iron, looked up by its symbol: 1 on the main menu, then 3, then Fe.
try     chemtab
run     clear
wait    1
run     chemtab
wait    4
send    \s
wait    2
send    n
wait    1
send    n
wait    4
send    1
wait    2
send    3
wait    1
send    Fe\r
wait    4
snap

shot    hp
size    24 80
cap     Reverse Polish in floating point: two plus three, times four; two to
cap     the tenth power; and a stack typed in, where P prints the values
cap     below the top and p the top.
try     hp 2 3 + 4 x; hp 2 10 :; echo "1.5 2.5 3.5 P + + p" | hp
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     hp 2 3 + 4 x
wait    3
run     hp 2 10 :
wait    3
run     echo "1.5 2.5 3.5 P + + p" | hp
wait    3
